:: R3-SideQuest-Instagram [release3 sidequest]
<<scene "img/ui/phone-instagram-wine.png" "Two wine glasses. One with a lipstick print.">>

3 AM. Still awake. Jess is breathing slow and deep beside me, face turned toward the wall. Her phone is on the nightstand, screen down.

I pick up my own phone and open Instagram. I don't follow Marcus. I've never searched for him. But the algorithm knows. It always knows. His profile appears in my suggestions feed like the universe nudging me toward a wound.

I tap.

His latest post is from yesterday. A gym selfie. Tank top, arms out, the kind of casual flex that looks unplanned but definitely isn't. Eight hundred forty seven likes. Comments, all fire emojis and heart eyes and women saying things that would make a sailor blush.

I scroll. More gym. A behind the scenes of his streaming setup. A sunset photo from his apartment balcony, the city spread out below him like something he owns.

Then I find it. Three posts back. A video of two takeout containers on a marble counter. His kitchen. Posted at 8 PM on Thursday. Two containers, two pairs of chopsticks, and in the background, slightly out of focus but unmistakable if you know what you're looking at, two wine glasses. One with a lipstick print on the rim.

Last Thursday. Jess said she was finishing up a proposal at Marcus's place. She came home at 10. Smelling like Thai food and Sauvignon Blanc.

I zoom in on the wine glasses. The lipstick shade is dark, berry something. Jess owns a lipstick called Blackberry Smash that she bought with her birthday money last year. I remember because she held it up in the store and said, //"This is the color of sophistication, babe."//

I'm zoom pinching on wine glass lipstick residue at 3 AM like a crime scene investigator who specializes in heartbreak. This is what I've become. A man lying next to his sleeping girlfriend, cataloging evidence he's not ready to confront.

I put the phone down. Pick it up. Put it down. Pick it up.

I screenshot the takeout photo. Save it to a folder. Then delete the folder. Then pull the screenshot from recently deleted. Then just turn off the phone and put it under the pillow and press my face into the mattress and breathe.

